solution:
To make the numerator rational, multiply by its conjugate, \(\sqrt3+\sqrt2\). This number is positive, so multiplying both numerator and denominator by it preserves the fraction:
\[
\frac{\sqrt3-\sqrt2}{\sqrt3}
=\frac{(\sqrt3-\sqrt2)(\sqrt3+\sqrt2)}{\sqrt3(\sqrt3+\sqrt2)}
=\frac{3-2}{3+\sqrt6}
=\frac1{3+\sqrt6}.
\]
The difference-of-squares identity removes the radicals from the numerator. Choice A gives the denominator before its multiplication is simplified; the requested simplest form is \(\boxed{3+\sqrt6}\), choice D.

